/*
Backtracking complexity depends on the number of valid paths explored, not just the input size.

Time Complexity: Exponential in the worst case, as all valid combinations are explored.
The time complexity is exponential in the worst case because the algorithm explores all valid combinations whose sum equals the target. 
At each recursive step, there are branching choices (pick or skip), and the recursion depth is bounded by how many times the smallest number 
can be added before exceeding the target. Since the number of valid combinations itself can be exponential, the overall time complexity is exponential.

Space Complexity:
- Auxiliary space: O(target / min(nums)) due to recursion depth.
- Output space: Exponential, proportional to the number of valid combinations.
The auxiliary space complexity is O(target / min(nums)), which comes from the maximum depth of the recursion stack and the temporary list used 
to store the current combination. Additionally, the output space is exponential, as all valid combinations must be stored in the result list.

INTUITION
Start with an empty combination and a sum of 0.

Look at the current number (nums[index]).

You have two choices:

Pick it → add it to the current set and increase the sum.

Skip it → move to the next number.

If the sum becomes exactly equal to the target:

You found a valid combination → save it.

If the sum becomes greater than the target:

This path can never work → stop exploring it.

Keep repeating this process until:

All valid combinations are found

Or you run out of numbers
*/
class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> combinationSum(int[] nums, int target) {
        List<List<Integer>> res = new ArrayList<>();
        List<Integer> curSet = new ArrayList();
        int index = 0,curSum=0;
        dfs(nums,index,curSum,curSet,res,target);
        return res;
    }
    public void dfs(int[] nums,int index,int curSum,List<Integer> curSet,List<List<Integer>> res,int target)
    {   
        if(curSum==target){
            res.add(new ArrayList<>(curSet));
            return;
        }
        if(curSum>target || index>=nums.length ){
            return;
        }
        curSet.add(nums[index]);
        dfs(nums,index,curSum+nums[index],curSet,res,target);
        curSet.remove(curSet.size()-1);
        dfs(nums,index+1,curSum,curSet,res,target);
    }
}

